<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ural Mountains</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ural Mountains are a north–south mountain range in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, traditionally regarded as the boundary between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> Russian expansion crossed the Urals into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, a process accelerated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yermak-timofeyevich">Yermak Timofeyevich</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> campaigns from 1581 onward. The region also became important for mining and metallurgy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ural-mountains.jpg" alt="Ural Mountains" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ugraland [1] from Moscow, Russia · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Landscape_view_in_Circumpolar_Urals.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By his death the state stretched from the White Sea to the steppe frontier south of the Oka and from Smolensk towards the <b>Urals</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Through tribute collected from Finnic and Ugric peoples and through trading expeditions, Novgorodians gathered furs, above all squirrel pelts, as well as wax, honey and walrus ivory from lands stretching to the White Sea, the Pechora and beyond the <b>Urals</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/novgorod-republic">Novgorod Republic · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…IV ('the Terrible'), who reigned from 1547 to 1584, was crowned Tsar and began the eastward expansion beyond the <b>Ural Mountains</b> into Siberia, a process accelerated by the Cossack campaigns of Yermak Timofeyevich from the early 1580s that would carry Russian sovereignty to the Pacific coast by 1639.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1928 Joseph Stalin’s five-year plans transformed the republic, which held most of the Soviet Union’s industry and resources: new industrial centres such as Magnitogorsk rose in the <b>Urals</b> and Siberia, cities grew rapidly and literacy became almost universal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Archaeologists trace early Sarmatian culture, often called the Prokhorovka culture, to the steppes of the southern <b>Urals</b> and the lower Volga in the fourth century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sarmatians">Sarmatians · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Russian explorers reached the Pacific at the Sea of Okhotsk in 1639, less than sixty years after Yermak’s cossacks first crossed the <b>Urals</b> into Siberia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
